t the airwaves  the show csi  las vegas miami new york  and cyber each episode  had key features that led to its  continued success  it all started with a crime or a series  of crimes  the csis arrived on scene in their  hummer and gathered evidence  typically in plain clothes the evidence  was returned to a state-of-the-art crime  lab full of future-esque  instrumentation the government labs  typically didn't have  or couldn't afford by the end of the  hour-long episode  they had analyzed all of the evidence  gathered solve the crime and made  arrests  the show created long lasting effects  for crime labs  investigators and prosecutors as we  tried to explain to juries of the  obstacles we have in testing every piece  of evidence  while struggling with rising backlogs  increased caseloads and the inability to  purchase some of the cutting-edge  instrumentation  presented in the show here's a brief  background  about arizona our state currently has a  population of about 7.6 million people  the department of public safety provides  lab services to more than 200 agencies  across 15 counties throughout our state  we also ranked 10th in violent crime  according to the 2018  fbi uniform crime reporting program  so what is allowed to do facing  increased dna analysis  requests and large backlogs  our lab superintendent developed an  operational vision of how our lab could  help assist investigations  and forge lasting partnerships with our  user agencies through the use of rapid  dna  if we could provide world-class customer  service and utilize emerging technology  we could also meet part of our mission  for the technical support division  at dps in june of 2013  our lab purchased rapid dna instruments  and a group of three people were tasked  with validating the instrument to  determine if it met certain requirements  for use  once it passed the validation we created  a training program  and implemented a law enforcement dna  program  through validation we were able to  determine that we could process single  source high quantity dna samples  while this limited some of the case  types and evidence we could use we were  still able to process homicide  aggravated assault and property crimes  each of the samples processed through  our rapid program  are submitted to an accredited crime  laboratory for confirmation  furthermore the operators cannot consume  any of the evidence as a part of this  program  in addition we created a standalone  database for the program  so operators could search for possible  candidates when a suspect standard was  not available  i can't cover every detail of our  program today so we can go over that in  a future talk  a lot of time was spent developing a  training program  and looking to see which agencies would  be interested  in being a part of our program we  offered it at no cost  to the agencies that were interested and  several agencies were ready to sign up  in may of 2014 we conducted our first  training class  we trained 10 operators during an  extensive 40-hour  hour class after  waiting and waiting in what seemed like  forever  the first case came in july of 2014.  i'd like to go over a quick recap of  some of our success  we have trained over 80 operators from  18 agencies  if you remember from earlier we service  over 200 agencies  through dps so that's why our program  requires each operator  to assist other agencies and process  those cases  so we can extend the impact of our  program  our operators have processed 531 cases  and aided 175 investigations  the program has even ruled out  investigative leads in some cases which  has allowed the subject to return to  their life and not be the focus of a  criminal investigation  the investigative leads generated  equates to a 33  hit rate i'd like to go through a few  case examples to illustrate the variety  of cases  and the impact to our community  the first case began during a traffic  stop in the middle of the night the  officer was verifying the information  from the driver and four other occupants  during this time one of the occupants  shot the officer in the face  while backup was already on the way the  suspect car circled around a building  to return more fire the backup officer  arrived on scene  an additional gun gunfire was exchanged  between the parties  the car was found abandoned early the  next morning with several bullet holes  there was blood in the rear seat of the  car where the suspect who had the gun  had been sitting near the car was a  bloody shirt with a hole through it  items were tested with rapid dna and the  dna profile obtained hit to a person in  our standalone database  they now knew who they were looking for  and found him about a week later  a jury found him guilty on seven charges  and he received a long sentence  the next case is a bank robbery from  southern arizona  the man you see in the slide walked into  a wells fargo bank  and made demands on a sheet of paper  indicating he had a weapon  after having his demands met he ran out  of the bank but left the sunglasses  you see behind the sunglasses were  tested  and the profile hit to a person in our  standalone database  police were able to apprehend him and he  was found guilty on felony robbery  charges  the next case is a child molestation  case out of the east valley  a family friend was in town for bike  week which is a motorcycle convention  that has concerts and rides  where some of the proceeds go to local  charities  the homeowners and their children went  to bed for the night and then the family  friend was found wandering the hallway  in the early morning when asked what he  was doing  he said he was looking for a towel for a  shower later that morning  one of the kids told their mother that  someone was in their room in the middle  of the night  another child revealed that he was in  her room touching her  after an examination of the room a  sample was tested from the sheets and  the seam and stain  matched the profile from the family  friend he entered a plea deal and was  sentenced to 10 years in prison  at the onset of the program we didn't  think we would be processing sexual  assault cases due to mixtures  however the type of stain produce a good  single source profile and we began  including cases where just semen was  recovered  the next case was partial remains  located in  a shed where blood was also found  samples from the scene were brought in  and tested the profile developed from  the blood stain  was compared to a secondary standard  from a missing person  in this case rapid dna aided in the  identification of this man and also  connected two agencies who work together  to solve the  homicide the final case is out of the  west valley  there have been several reports in the  neighborhood of a peeping tom looking in  windows  while the occupants called police the  individual always eluded them  on one night the behavior of this  individual escalated and he broke in  through a window  while the female resident was taking a  shower he stood in the doorway and  watched her shower when she got out of  the shower there was a struggle between  them  but the suspect fled out of the back  door after he was gone  she noticed that there was semen on her  hand the sample was collected  and tested on the rapid dna instrument  the dna profile that was obtained hit  to a person on our standalone database  the suspect was apprehended  and lived a quarter of a mile from the  victim  this program continues to provide  world-class customer service and timely  investigative information to assist in  quicker apprehension of criminals  in addition results generated from some  cases has excluded individuals from the  case  and allowed them to return to their life  in a much quicker timeline than afforded  by  traditional investigations in turn  detectives can refocus their  investigation to identify those involved  in the crime  the operators we have trained to use  this pro